  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 246 PM EDT SUN SEP 27 2026\n\nWhile surface high pressure will remain over eastern Kentucky \nyielding continued sunny skies Tuesday, warm advection will begin\nto gradually increase through midweek as upper ridging builds \nover the Ohio Valley. Meanwhile, a cut-off low over the southwest \nCONUS will advect deep tropical moisture northward from the \nsubtropical eastern Pacific Ocean into the middle of the country \nahead of a cold front over the Southern Plains, with models \nstruggling significantly in the evolution of this low as the week \nprogresses, causing increasing uncertainty downstream over eastern\nKentucky. At the same time, model ensemble systems also differ on\nthe speed, location, and strength of a northern stream system and\nassociated cold front traversing the northern part of the \ncountry, especially as it moves across the Great Lakes and Ohio \nValley, later this week. \n\nWhile there is significant model uncertainty with how these two key \nfeatures evolve, confidence is high in a significant warm-up from \nTuesday through Thursday, with highs peaking Thursday in the mid to \nupper 80s, before a cooling trend begins Friday as clouds and rain \nchances begin to increase from the west and/or southwest. Whenever \nrain does arrive Friday and/or this weekend, there is a good chance \nthat it will be accompanied by some degree of tropical fetch off of \nthe subtropical eastern Pacific and/or the Gulf of America, which \ncould yield (40-50% chance) rain amounts exceeding 0.5\" in a 24-hour \nperiod.",
